.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:107:- **`routes/admin.ts` isolated:** The backfill endpoint is operationally different (run once, dangerous if re-triggered against live data) — keeping it in its own file makes it easy to gate behind an extra check (e.g., a shared-secret header) or delete after initial launch.
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:271:**Why it's wrong:** Makes it hard to add filters/validation consistently, and hard to test routes without a live D1 binding.
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:288:| Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust) | Configured at the zone/hostname level in the Cloudflare dashboard, in front of the Worker's custom domain | Not app code — see Anti-Pattern 4. One-Time-PIN login method, single-email allow policy. |
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:294:| Frontend (public/*.js) ↔ API (src/routes/*) | `fetch()` over same-origin JSON, envelope `{ ok, data }` / `{ ok:false, error }` | Same-origin because both are served by the same Worker — no CORS configuration needed at all. |
.planning/research/PITFALLS.md:15:Binance geo-blocks a list of "Restricted Locations" (US, Ontario, etc.) and, independently, has a long history of blocking known **datacenter/cloud IP ranges** outright regardless of declared location, because bots and arbitrage scrapers abuse them. Cloudflare Workers share IP pools across thousands of unrelated accounts/colocations, so which colo (and therefore which apparent country/ASN) handles a given `fetch()` is non-deterministic per-request. This is a well-documented, recurring problem reported on both the Cloudflare community forum ("Can't fetch Binance api") and Binance's own developer forum ("Can't fetch API from Cloudflare worker") — it is not a hypothetical edge case, it happens in practice and can be intermittent (works today, breaks tomorrow when traffic routes through a different edge node/IP).
.planning/research/PITFALLS.md:37:The plan's architecture diagram shows a single "Workers (API)" box handling both HTTP routes and the cron sync. If the project is deployed as a Cloudflare **Pages** project (as implied by `pages.dev` URLs and static `public/` hosting) with `functions/` for the API, the cron sync will never run — Pages Functions cannot register Cron Triggers. This is confirmed directly by Cloudflare community threads and is a common "worked in Workers, broken in Pages" trap.
.planning/research/PITFALLS.md:165:| Single combined Worker doing both API routes and cron | Fewer moving parts to reason about initially | Breaks entirely once deployed via Pages (Pitfall 2) or once cron work grows past CPU budget shared with API traffic | Never — split from Phase 1 |
.planning/research/PITFALLS.md:231:| Access not covering API routes | Phase 5 | Unauthenticated curl against `/api/records` and `/api/klines` is rejected after Access setup |
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:40:- Cloudflare Access password gate covering both the UI and the API routes
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:53:Single Cloudflare Worker project: static assets (`public/`) served free at the edge, a Hono-based `fetch()` router for the 5 API routes plus a cursor-paginated admin backfill endpoint, and a `scheduled()` handler for the daily incremental sync — all sharing one D1 binding and deployed via one `wrangler.jsonc`/`wrangler deploy`. All SQL lives behind a `lib/db.ts` repository layer; the Binance fetch/normalize/backoff logic lives in `lib/binance.ts` and is shared between the backfill and cron paths (cron's "delta since last close" is a strict subset of backfill's "paginate until done" logic).
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:57:2. API router (`fetch` handler + `routes/*.ts`) — parses/validates requests, calls the DB layer, returns a `{ok, data|error}` JSON envelope
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:86:**Uses:** Hono API routes from Phase 1, Zod validation
.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:54:- [ ] **INFRA-02**: API routes use Hono router with JSON envelope response format (`{ok, data|error}`)
.planning/phases/05-records-filtering-time-entry-ux/5-SUMMARY.md:13:| `src/routes/records.ts` | GET `/api/records` Zod-validates `c.req.query()` via `listRecordsQuerySchema` before any DB call; invalid type → 400 envelope. |
===ACCESS in ARCH===
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:17:│                     Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust)                  │
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:53:Everything — HTML/CSS/JS, the JSON API, and the cron sync job — deploys as one `wrangler deploy` from one `wrangler.jsonc`/`wrangler.toml`. Cloudflare Access is configured at the zone/hostname level, in front of the whole Worker, so it does not need to be implemented in application code at all.
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:61:| DB access layer (`lib/db.ts`) | All D1 queries live here; nothing else touches `env.DB` directly | Thin repository-style module, one function per query shape |
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:68:| Cloudflare Access | Auth gate in front of the whole app | Zero Trust dashboard config, not app code |
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:106:- **`lib/db.ts` as sole D1 access point:** Keeps SQL in one place so schema changes (e.g., adding a column) touch one file, and makes it easy to unit-test route handlers by mocking the DB layer instead of a real D1 binding.
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:247:| If ever multi-user | Would require an `owner_id` column on `divergence_records`, auth beyond Cloudflare Access (real per-user identity), and rate limiting on write endpoints — out of scope for this project by explicit decision. |
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:278:**Do this instead:** Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust) in front of the whole hostname, with a One-Time-PIN policy restricted to the owner's email — zero application code, verified pattern from the owner's existing `soapwavehealing` project experience.
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:288:| Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust) | Configured at the zone/hostname level in the Cloudflare dashboard, in front of the Worker's custom domain | Not app code — see Anti-Pattern 4. One-Time-PIN login method, single-email allow policy. |
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:306:5. **Cloudflare Access last, but cheap.** It's dashboard configuration, not code — sequencing it after the app works avoids fighting the auth gate during local development (`wrangler dev` is unaffected by production Access policies anyway, so this is more about not forgetting it before going "live" with a public repo).
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:317:- [Publish a self-hosted application to the Internet · Cloudflare One docs](https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/access-controls/applications/http-apps/self-hosted-public-app/) — HIGH confidence, official docs
.planning/research/ARCHITECTURE.md:318:- [Add web applications · Cloudflare One docs](https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/access-controls/applications/http-apps/) — HIGH confidence, official docs
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:14:Recommended approach: build the D1 schema and a single Worker (assets + API router + cron) first, prove the Binance fetch works from a deployed (not local-dev) Worker and that chunked D1 batch-inserts work at real 1000-row page scale, then layer Records UI → Chart UI → Calculator → Cloudflare Access in that order, matching both the feature dependency graph and the owner's stated priority.
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:20:Cloudflare Workers with the `assets` binding (not a separate Pages project) serving three static HTML pages plus a Hono-routed JSON API and a `scheduled()` cron handler, backed by Cloudflare D1 (SQLite) for the `klines` and `divergence_records` tables. Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust, email OTP) provides the auth gate with zero application code. TradingView Lightweight Charts v5 (UMD/standalone build, loaded via CDN, no build step) renders the candlesticks; Zod validates API request bodies at the boundary.
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:28:- Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust): password/auth gate configured at the dashboard/zone level, not in app code
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:40:- Cloudflare Access password gate covering both the UI and the API routes
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:58:3. DB repository (`lib/db.ts`) — sole point of D1 access; chunked `db.batch()` inserts for bulk data, `INSERT OR IGNORE` keyed on `(symbol, open_time)` for idempotent syncs
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:61:6. Cloudflare Access — auth gate configured at the zone/hostname level, covering both UI and `/api/*`, with no application code
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:102:### Phase 5: Access & Launch Hardening
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:103:**Rationale:** Auth is an infrastructure concern with no feature dependencies and doesn't interfere with local development (`wrangler dev` bypasses production Access policies), so it's cheapest to add last, right before going live with what may be a public repo.
.planning/research/SUMMARY.md:104:**Delivers:** Cloudflare Access application configured at the zone/hostname level (One-Time-PIN, single-email allow policy) covering both the static UI and all `/api/*` routes; verified via unauthenticated curl test.
